// The repo's declared invariants — the manifest the git-gate compiles.
//
//   koruc invariants/invariants.kz invariants          # list
//   python3 invariants/gate.py                         # the gate consumer:
//     per git-gate row — run `check:` if declared (deterministic, blocks);
//     no `check:` → judge the staged diff via the gate binary (koru/odds).
//
// koruc emits its backend artifacts next to the entry file; this directory
// exists so they land here (gitignored) and not in the repo root, which
// carries a tracked, hand-written build.zig. Checks run with the compilation
// root — this directory — as cwd, so repo paths start with `..`.

~import std/invariants
// Pulled in for its colocated git-gate-local declarations — the listing
// walks imports, so a module's own invariants surface only through one.
~import koru/odds

~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "emit-for-not-while",
    "tags": ["git-gate"],
    "rule": "Any emitter producing a counted Zig loop emits for, not while. while (i < len) : (i += 1) hands the optimizer a mutable induction variable and a loop-carried condition; for (0..len) |i| hands it a known trip count, and for (xs, ys) |*x, y| additionally proves non-aliasing and removes bounds checks — that is what lets LLVM vectorize. A while is legitimate only where the trip count genuinely is not known up front: a parser scan, a freelist walk, an early-breaking search. The check decides over the string-literal content of koru_std transforms — the text that BECOMES emitted Zig (allocPrint formats with escapes decoded, appendSlice literals, multiline blocks) — and never flags a transform's own compile-time loops. Every emitted while is a violation unless it carries a declared exemption (file + condition fingerprint + reason) in checks/emitted-while.allow; a stale exemption fails the check. The check self-tests against must-match and must-not-match fixtures before scanning and reports BROKEN, never clean, if it cannot prove it can still match. Out of its reach, decided by inference at review time: bare Zig in runtime proc bodies (ships verbatim, indistinguishable from compile-time code without annotation context) and the emitter templates in src/.",
    "check": "python3 checks/check_emitted_while.py ../koru_std checks/emitted-while.allow"
}

~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "call-valued-index",
    "tags": ["git-gate"],
    "rule": "Emitted Zig never puts a call expression in index position against a named base: store.px[store.__koru_resolve(h)] is not an indexed load — the base is an aggregate held by value in a mutable global and the index is a call that could mutate that global, so Zig materialises the whole aggregate before indexing. Measured 2026-07-31: 80 KB copied per access at store capacity 10,000, ~100% of sweep runtime in _platform_memmove, 1750x recovered by binding the call to a const first (concepts/frag-a-call-valued-index-copies-the-whole-array.md). Every such site is CORRECT, so no test can see it. What the check actually catches — the honest heuristic subset: in the string-literal content of koru_std transforms, an index or slice bracket whose content contains a non-builtin call, with a named chain (identifiers, fields, placeholders) as base. It cannot see types, so it over-approximates beyond by-value aggregates on purpose — the hoist fix is correct for every base. It cannot see calls assembled across concatenated fragments, calls arriving through a {s} placeholder's runtime value, or bare proc-body Zig. Exemptions in checks/call-valued-index.allow, same mechanics as emitted-while; the check self-tests before scanning and reports BROKEN, never clean, if its patterns stop matching.",
    "check": "python3 checks/check_call_valued_index.py ../koru_std checks/call-valued-index.allow"
}

~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "every-emitted-variant-has-a-witness",
    "tags": ["post-full-run"],
    "rule": "Every variant an emitter can produce must appear in at least one test's emitted output; a variant appearing in zero is invisible surface — it ships, no test has ever run it, and a green board says nothing about it (__kz_wd, the raw_posix integer digit writer, was found by hand this way). This is a different axis from the two checks above: they are static over emitter source (what the emitter COULD write); this one is dynamic over emitted artifacts (what the corpus actually MADE it write), so it runs in the post-full-run slot beside the wall census, where artifacts exist for the whole board. The variant set is DERIVED, never hand-listed: a variant is identified by its marker, a __-prefixed symbol in the emitted-string content of the koru_std transforms (same extraction as the sibling checks), with template placeholders as wildcards and concatenation-head fragments folded into their templated duals. Coverage reads each test's output_emitted.zig and counts a marker only where it appears as BARE code — emitted text lives inside string literals at the emitter and materialises as bare code in the artifact, while emitter source embedded in an artifact keeps its markers inside strings, so the bare/quoted inversion is what makes a witness a witness. Artifacts are ephemeral: below half the test dirs carrying one (a full board measures ~65%, a filtered run ~1%) the check reports BROKEN and voids its verdict, never clean. The claim is deliberately narrow and named: a variant that emits only anonymous code shares its witness with every sibling using the same helper symbols; the |js variant (console.log into output_emitted.js), src/ emitter templates, prose-quoted symbols, and variants reachable only under another --build host are out of reach — an uncovered symbol whose invisibility is understood gets a reasoned exemption in checks/variant-coverage.allow, and a stale exemption fails. The check self-tests derivation and artifact scanning against must-match and must-not-match fixtures before judging.",
    "check": "python3 checks/check_variant_coverage.py ../koru_std ../tests/regression checks/variant-coverage.allow"
}

// The judgment class: rules with no `check:` are routed by the gate to a
// reader — Jev via koru/odds — against the staged diff. The `rule` text is
// written to BE the question: diff-scoped, verdict-shaped, self-contained.
~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "no-fallbacks",
    "tags": ["git-gate"],
    "rule": "Does this diff introduce a fallback — a degraded path a reader would mistake for the real surface working? A fallback is: a default value substituted for a failed fetch or computation, placeholder output standing where real content belongs, a swallowed error converted into a silent skip or empty result, or a second code path labeled 'for now', 'minimum', 'if X fails', or 'fallback'. Designed states are NOT fallbacks: an empty state that says it is empty, a documented default parameter, an idle pose, a named and tested degraded mode. Answer yes ONLY if the diff adds or keeps a substitute output that hides the real path's failure or absence."
}

// Syntax rulings, gated: a shipped spelling nobody ruled is the failure this
// row exists for (ref(T) entered the stdlib on inherited vocabulary, caught
// only when Lars asked — confession udue5gx). The concept being discussed in
// a design note is not a ruling on its spelling; the gate asks about the
// spelling specifically.
~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "surface-spellings-are-ruled",
    "tags": ["git-gate"],
    "rule": "Does this diff add recognition for a new user-facing spelling — a shape a user can write that the toolchain detects by pattern — with no recorded ruling? Spellings include: type-position forms matched in field or argument text (`ref(T)`, `?T`, `<:`), new `std/` tor or proc names, and marker conventions downstream code string-matches. A ruling is recorded: a commit message, a design doc's RULED line, or a concepts/ frag naming the spelling as decided. A concept being ranked or a semantics being discussed is NOT a ruling on its spelling. Flag when the diff teaches koru_std or src/ to recognize or lower a form no ruling names. Do NOT flag: internal `__koru_*` machinery, diagnostic message text, refactors of ruled spellings, or a spelling whose ruling is recorded in this same diff. Answer yes ONLY if the diff creates public surface whose spelling nobody ruled."
}

~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "claim-stamps-stay-honest",
    "tags": ["git-gate"],
    "rule": "Does this diff weaken a std/rules claim silently? Declarations carry claim stamps — proven, measured, inferred, aspirational — visible inline in the source this diff touches. Flag when the diff edits the body of a declaration stamped proven or measured without the diff also touching the evidence the stamp rests on (a test, benchmark, or proof artifact), or when it removes or downgrades a stamp without saying so. Editing a claimed declaration together with its evidence, or strengthening a stamp, is NOT a violation. Answer yes ONLY if the diff leaves a stamp asserting something the edit has made untrue."
}

// The semantic half of the ref gap — narrowed, not closed. Both
// consumers that exist check at their own boundary through one shared
// authority (StoreRefHome.homesOf): stores at column writes
// (698_012-016), lists at push (698_017 traps, 698_018 refuses the
// homeless). What remains debt is structural: nothing FORCES the next
// consumer to ask — a grid or table could still lower `ref(T)` to bare
// i64 and check nothing. Declared aspirational because the real fix may
// be ref-ness in the type environment — undecided, Lars's call.
~std/invariants:aspirational {
    "name": "ref-provenance-every-consumer",
    "tags": ["review"],
    "rule": "Every consumer that materializes `ref(T)` fields enforces handle provenance — the value's brand must trace to a plural, handle-minting store whose expanded field set subsumes T, via the shared StoreRefHome.homesOf enumeration. std/store checks at every write path (insert, apply, envelope, bulk); std/list checks at push and refuses a homeless ref at new. Flag when a diff adds a consumer that materializes `ref(T)` fields (element structs, cell types, columns) without routing the field through the home check — a spelling that checks in one consumer and not another is a type that lies about its coverage. Whether ref-ness moves into the type environment so checking is unskippable remains undecided."
}

// The manifest guards itself: a git-gate-local row fires only when its
// declaring file is in the staged diff, and is judged against that
// file's hunks alone. This row is where weakening the discipline would
// land — and it is judged exactly then.
~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "manifest-stays-enforceable",
    "tags": ["git-gate-local"],
    "rule": "Does this diff weaken the invariant manifest's ability to catch what it declares? Flag when it removes or renames a tag so a row stops firing, softens a rule's verdict shape (rules are questions a judge answers yes/no about the diff), drops a check: command without a stronger replacement, or adds a git-gate-local row whose declared file the listing cannot place. Narrowing an over-broad rule with the reason stated in the rule text, adding rows, or strengthening a check is NOT a violation. Answer yes ONLY if the diff makes the manifest weaker at catching what it declares."
}

// The rename residue rule: `event` → `tor` reached code but not prose
// (swept 2026-09-21 — ~446 comment lines across koru_std + by-example).
// Diff-scoped so the untouched backlog (internal vocabulary, diagnostics,
// dated docs) never fires it; only NEW prose can regress.
~std/invariants:inferred {
    "name": "the-construct-is-a-tor",
    "tags": ["git-gate"],
    "rule": "Does this diff add prose — a comment, a doc, or a string a user reads — that names the language's declared unit 'event' or 'events'? The unit is a tor: `pub tor` declares it, `|` arms end it, `!` effect arms hang off it, `|>` continues it. Flag added text like 'the event's branches', 'pub event foo', 'events are tappable'. Do NOT flag: internal identifiers and generated names (EventDecl, event_decl, *_event, created_by_event, event-denied, or a test's own names like void-event); domain events — DOM, terminal, socket, input, key/mouse events, an event loop, emitted log records such as 'transition events'; verbatim quotes of older behavior; and meta-discussion of this rename itself. Answer yes ONLY if the added prose would teach a reader that the construct's name is 'event'."
}

~tor main {}

~proc main|zig {
}

~main()
